NVIDIA Denver CPU:
Worked in the MTS Team that maintains a JIT to translate ARM instructions into a native VLIW ISA and extract better ILP via Dynamic Code Optimizations and Speculative Rollbacks. Added support for the following features
+ Branch assert instruction to remove cold branches and improve Instruction Scheduling
+ Lazy Branch Asserts to avoid remove asserting branch instructions unless the asserts are taken frequently enough.
+ Branch Reprofiling to detect dynamic changes in branch weights of a running program and trigger Recompilation. libquantum showed ~50% improvement.
+ Elide Memory fence instructions. This allowed more aggresive Instruction scheduling. Transaction was rolled back if a concurrent commit from another core accessed speculative lines. Helps JVM workloads.

Also maintained local patches to Tegra Linux kernel to optimize for Denver. Changes included:
+ Large Page Support for ARMv7. Used a BSD like Lazy Page Coalescing approach to collapse adjacent small pages into a large page silently in the HAL layer of the kernel.
+ Thread Local option in madvise and corresponding Kernel MM support. Descriptors marked local can elide TLB invalidates, Resulting IPIs and cache snooping since such accesses cannot be made from another core concurrently. On Thread Migration, a global TLB invalidate performed for Threads that access this memory.

